# JIRA-to-Exchange RAML Publish Workflow

Execute all phases below automatically when the user provides JIRA story IDs or a local spec folder name. Do not stop to ask unless a value is genuinely ambiguous after reading all sources.

**Trigger phrases:**
- `publish raml <JIRA-ID> [<JIRA-ID>...]` — full end-to-end (Phases 1-8)
- `publish exchange <spec-folder-name>` — skip to Phase 6 if RAML already exists locally

---

## Phase 1 — Read Standards First (parallel)

Before touching any files, read these two rules simultaneously:
- `gen-raml-implementation.mdc` — RAML structure, naming conventions, CommonLib usage, mandatory patterns
- `review-api-design-standards.mdc` — validation checklist to apply before finishing

---

## Phase 2 — Fetch Each JIRA Story

For each story ID, call the JIRA REST API:

```
GET https://insulet.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/{STORY-ID}
Authorization: Basic base64(email:token)
Accept: application/json
```

**Credentials**: Read from `~/.cursor/mcp.json` → `mcpServers.atlassian-guardrails.envFile`

To locate the .env file, read `~/.cursor/mcp.json` and extract the `envFile` path for the `atlassian-guardrails` MCP server. Required variables:
- `JIRA_EMAIL` — account email
- `JIRA_TOKEN` — API token

Fields to extract:

| Field | JSON key |
|---|---|
| Summary | `fields.summary` |
| User story | `fields.description` |
| Acceptance Criteria | `fields.customfield_10308` |
| Technical Notes (Confluence URL lives here) | `fields.customfield_10364` |

Parse Atlassian Document Format (ADF) nodes to plain text. Confluence URLs appear as `inlineCard` nodes or plain `text` nodes inside `customfield_10364`.

---

## Phase 3 — Fetch the Confluence Design Page

Extract the Confluence page URL from Technical Notes. URL format:
```
https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
```

Fetch with the same credentials:
```
GET https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/rest/api/content/{PAGE-ID}?expand=body.storage
Authorization: Basic base64(email:token)
Accept: application/json
```

Strip HTML from `body.storage.value` and extract:
- Endpoint paths (e.g., `GET /consents`, `PUT /consents/{id}`)
- Query parameters, URI parameters, request body fields (name, type, mandatory flag)
- Output field mappings — API field name, data type, Salesforce source field, description
- HTTP status codes and their trigger conditions
- Salesforce object name (e.g., `ContactPointTypeConsent`)

If multiple JIRA stories reference the same Confluence page, fetch it only once.

---

## Phase 4 — Resolve Design Decisions (automatic, no user prompt)

Apply these precedence rules in order:

| Decision | Rule |
|---|---|
| Field naming conflict between JIRA TC Guide and Confluence column header | **JIRA TC Guide wins** — e.g., if TC Guide says "Map Name to `consentName`", use `consentName` even if the Confluence column header says `name` |
| Response envelope | **Always `data`** as the top-level wrapper per `gen-raml-implementation.mdc` §5 — regardless of what Confluence shows |
| HTTP verb | **Follow Confluence/JIRA exactly** — do not silently change PUT to PATCH or vice versa |
| PUT endpoints | **Inline body/responses** — `CommonLib.generic` defines `get?`, `post?`, `patch?` only; for PUT, define the method explicitly with `is: [CommonLib.common-error-handler, CommonLib.common-headers, CommonLib.system-headers]` |
| Fields with no Salesforce mapping | **Remove them** — do not include fields that have no source field in the Confluence output table |
| Extra fields not in Confluence output (e.g., `referenceId`) | **Remove them** |
| `data` wrapper for Salesforce update responses | Apply `data` object wrapper with `id` and `success` |

---

## Phase 5 — Generate or Update RAML

**Output location:** `.cursor/rules/API Reusable Asset/{api-spec-name}/`

Derive `api-spec-name` from Confluence/JIRA using the `{name}-{layer}-api-spec` pattern (no regional prefix).

- **Spec already exists** → update files in place, remove stale fields/DataTypes not in the current Confluence design
- **Spec does not exist** → scaffold the folder structure with an empty `exchange_modules/` directory — Phase 7 will download and populate it from Exchange (Phase 7 always runs for new specs)

### Folder structure

```
{api-spec-name}/
├── {api-spec-name}.raml          ← root RAML
├── dataTypes/
│   └── {DataTypeName}.raml       ← one file per type (TitleCase)
├── examples/
│   └── {ExampleName}Example.raml ← one file per example (TitleCase)
└── exchange_modules/             ← populated from Exchange in Phase 7 (always runs for new specs)
```

### Root RAML patterns

**Security — always use library reference, never a direct file include:**
```yaml
securedBy:
  - CommonLib.client-id-scheme
```

**`CommonLib.generic` for POST — include all required params (v1.0.14+ requires `postResponseType`):**
```yaml
type:
  CommonLib.generic:
    postDescription: "..."
    postBodyType: MyRequestType
    postBodyExample: !include examples/MyRequestExample.raml
    postResponseExample: !include examples/MyResponseExample.raml
    postResponseType: MyResponseType
```

**PUT endpoints — inline, not via CommonLib.generic:**
```yaml
/{id}:
  put:
    description: "..."
    body:
      application/json:
        type: MyUpdateRequest
        example: !include examples/MyUpdateRequestExample.raml
    responses:
      201:
        body:
          application/json:
            type: MyUpdateResponse
            example: !include examples/MyUpdateResponseExample.raml
  is: [CommonLib.common-error-handler, CommonLib.common-headers, CommonLib.system-headers]
```

### Phase 5 checklist

- [ ] All business endpoints use `CommonLib.generic` (except PUT — inline)
- [ ] `CommonLib.common-error-handler` applied to every endpoint via `is:`
- [ ] No manually defined 4xx/5xx responses in root RAML
- [ ] Every response DataType and example has a top-level `data` wrapper
- [ ] Example files are TitleCase (e.g., `ConsentsGetResponseExample.raml`)
- [ ] No regional prefix in spec name
- [ ] `baseUri` declared
- [ ] `/ping` and `/health-check` included using `CommonLib.liveliness-probe` / `CommonLib.readiness-probe`
- [ ] `securedBy: [CommonLib.client-id-scheme]` (not a direct file include)
- [ ] `postResponseType` supplied wherever `CommonLib.generic` is used with `post?`

---

## Phase 6 — Check Latest common-fragment-library Version

Before publishing, always verify the local `exchange_modules/` is on the latest version.

Query Exchange:
```
GET /exchange/api/v2/assets
  ?organizationId={BU_GROUP_ID}
  &search=common-fragment-library
  &types=raml-fragment
Authorization: Bearer {token}
```

**Credentials**: Read from `~/.cursor/mcp.json` → `mcpServers.anypoint-guardrails.envFile`

To locate the .env file, read `~/.cursor/mcp.json` and extract the `envFile` path for the `anypoint-guardrails` MCP server. Required variables:
- `ANYPOINT_CLIENT_ID`, `ANYPOINT_CLIENT_SECRET`
- `ANYPOINT_BU_GROUP_ID` = `e0bc8c96-9dbd-48bc-915e-3e29577af6b3`

Get the Bearer token first:
```
POST /accounts/api/v2/oauth2/token
grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=...&client_secret=...
```

The `version` field on the returned asset is the latest. If it is newer than what is in the local `exchange_modules/` folder, proceed to Phase 7.

---

## Phase 7 — Upgrade exchange_modules and Root RAML References

### Download the latest common-fragment-library zip

```
GET /exchange/api/v2/assets/{BU_GROUP_ID}/common-fragment-library/{LATEST_VERSION}
→ find file with classifier=raml-fragment, packaging=zip
→ download from the S3 externalLink WITHOUT Authorization header (pre-signed URL)
```

### Replace local exchange_modules

```
# Remove old version folder
rm -rf exchange_modules/{BU_GROUP_ID}/common-fragment-library/{OLD_VERSION}/

# Extract new version into
exchange_modules/{BU_GROUP_ID}/common-fragment-library/{LATEST_VERSION}/
```

### Update all version references in the spec

1. In `{api-spec-name}.raml` — update `uses:` path:
   ```yaml
   uses:
     CommonLib: exchange_modules/{BU}/{assetId}/{LATEST_VERSION}/common-fragment-library.raml
   ```
2. In `exchange.json` — update the dependency version:
   ```json
   "dependencies": [{ "version": "{LATEST_VERSION}", ... }]
   ```
3. Scan the new `resourceTypes/resourceType.raml` for any new required parameters. For each `CommonLib.generic` usage in the root RAML, add any missing required parameter (e.g., `postResponseType`, `getResponseType`).

### Verify no `1.0.2` references remain
```bash
grep -r "{OLD_VERSION}" .  # must return no matches
```

---

## Phase 8 — Publish via Design Center (Editable Exchange Asset)

Publishing directly to Exchange (raw multipart API) creates a **read-only** asset with no Design Center project link. Always publish via Design Center to produce an editable asset.

### Critical API facts

| Fact | Detail |
|---|---|
| `x-owner-id` header | Required on all Design Center write calls — client_credentials tokens carry no `userId` claim. Resolve from `GET /accounts/api/me` → `user.id` |
| Branch lock | Must call `acquireLock` before `save`, and always call `releaseLock` in a finally block |
| No exchange_modules in upload | Upload only RAML files + `exchange.json`. Design Center resolves CommonLib dependency from Exchange automatically |
| `exchange.json` name alignment | The `name` field in `exchange.json` must exactly match the `name` value sent in the publish call |
| Use the spec owner's user ID | The project owner in Design Center must be the logged-in Anypoint user — use their ID as `x-owner-id` |
| **Metadata in exchange.json** | **REQUIRED** — `exchange.json` must contain `metadata` block with `projectId`, `branchId`, and `commitId` before publishing. Fetch commitId after each save and update exchange.json accordingly |
| Lock for publish | Must re-acquire lock before calling publish/exchange endpoint — publish requires an active lock |

### API call sequence

All calls use these headers unless noted:
```
Authorization: Bearer {token}
x-organization-id: {BU_GROUP_ID}
x-owner-id: {userId}            ← resolved from /accounts/api/me
Content-Type: application/json
```

**Step 8.1 — Resolve owner user ID**
```
GET /accounts/api/me
→ response.user.id
```

**Step 8.2 — Create Design Center project**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects
Body: { "name": "{api-spec-name}", "type": "raml" }
→ response.id = {project_id}
```

**Step 8.3 — Acquire branch lock**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/acquireLock
Body: {}
→ { "locked": true, "name": "..." }
```

**Step 8.4 — Upload RAML files (initial save)**

Files to include (skip `exchange_modules/` entirely):
- `{api-spec-name}.raml`
- `dataTypes/*.raml` (all files)
- `examples/*.raml` (all files)
- `exchange.json` (with placeholder metadata — will be updated in Step 8.6)

```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/save
Body: [
  { "path": "{api-spec-name}.raml", "content": "..." },
  { "path": "dataTypes/MyType.raml", "content": "..." },
  { "path": "examples/MyExample.raml", "content": "..." },
  { "path": "exchange.json", "content": "..." }
]
```

**Step 8.5 — Fetch commitId and update exchange.json with complete metadata**

After the initial save, fetch the latest commitId from Design Center:
```
GET /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/commits?limit=1
→ response[0].id = {commitId}
```

Update the local `exchange.json` to include the complete `metadata` block:
```json
{ 
  "main": "{api-spec-name}.raml",
  "name": "{Human Readable API Name}",
  "classifier": "raml",
  ...
  "metadata": {
    "projectId": "{project_id}",
    "branchId": "master",
    "commitId": "{commitId}"
  }
}
```

Save the updated `exchange.json` to Design Center:
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/save
Body: [
  { "path": "exchange.json", "content": "{updated exchange.json with metadata}" }
]
```

Fetch the new commitId after this save (the metadata save creates a new commit):
```
GET /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/commits?limit=1
→ response[0].id = {final_commitId}
```

Update the local `exchange.json` with the final commitId for local consistency.

**Step 8.6 — Release branch lock (always, even on error)**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/releaseLock
Body: {}
→ { "locked": false }
```

**Step 8.7 — Acquire lock for publish**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/acquireLock
Body: {}
→ { "locked": true, "name": "..." }
```

**Step 8.8 — Publish to Exchange**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/publish/exchange
Body: {
  "name": "{Human Readable API Name}",
  "main": "{api-spec-name}.raml",
  "apiVersion": "v1",
  "version": "{semver e.g. 1.0.0}",
  "assetId": "{api-spec-name}",
  "groupId": "{BU_GROUP_ID}",
  "classifier": "raml"
}
```

On success (HTTP 200) the asset is live at:
```
https://anypoint.mulesoft.com/exchange/{BU_GROUP_ID}/{api-spec-name}/{version}/
```

**Step 8.9 — Release lock after publish**
```
POST /designcenter/api-designer/projects/{project_id}/branches/master/releaseLock
Body: {}
→ { "locked": false }
```

### Version strategy

If the asset already exists on Exchange from a previous raw API publish (read-only), bump the version by one patch (e.g., existing `1.0.0` → publish as `1.0.1` from Design Center). The Design Center publish will add the editable version alongside the existing read-only one. The read-only version cannot be deleted via API (Exchange DELETE is not supported for `rest-api` assets).

### Reusable MCP tool

The `anypoint-guardrails` MCP server exposes `anypoint_create_and_publish_design_center` which wraps Phase 8 steps 8.1-8.6 as a single tool call:

```
anypoint_create_and_publish_design_center(
  project_name    = "{api-spec-name}",
  spec_folder_path= "/abs/path/to/{api-spec-name}/",
  asset_id        = "{api-spec-name}",
  asset_version   = "1.0.0",
  display_name    = "{Human Readable Name}",
  description     = "...",
  api_version     = "v1",
  main_file       = "{api-spec-name}.raml"
)
```

The tool automatically excludes `exchange_modules/`, acquires and releases the lock, and returns the Exchange URL on success.

